-- Q1174: Legal Duties and Responsibilities [hard/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both positions meet the applicable standard because ''substantial authority'' (approximately 40%) applies uniformly to all return positions regardless of transaction type","The non-shelter position meets ''substantial authority'' at 45% likelihood, but the tax shelter position fails because it must meet the ''more likely than not'' standard (greater than 50%), reflecting Congress''s heightened scrutiny of shelter transactions","Tax shelter positions have a lower standard (''reasonable basis'') because shelters involve complex legal issues that inherently carry lower certainty of success","Both positions automatically fail because any position with less than 75% likelihood triggers the Section 6694(a) penalty for all transaction types"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 6694(a)(2)(C) imposes the "more likely than not" standard (greater than 50% likelihood) for tax shelter positions, compared to "substantial authority" (approximately 40%) for non-shelter positions. At 45% likelihood, the non-shelter position clears its threshold while the identical shelter position falls short. Congress enacted the higher standard to curb abusive tax shelters.
Wrong (A): The standards differ based on whether the transaction is a tax shelter; "substantial authority" is not uniform.
Wrong (C): This reverses the relationship — shelter positions face higher standards, not lower.
Wrong (D): No 75% threshold exists in the Section 6694 penalty provisions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1174;

-- Q1177: Debtor-Creditor Relationships [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Filing a financing statement with the Secretary of State, which is required for perfection but not attachment","The debtor making the first payment on the secured loan, which demonstrates performance but is not an attachment requirement","The creditor obtaining a court judgment against the debtor, which is a remedy available after default but not an attachment step","The secured party giving value, the debtor having rights in the collateral, and an authenticated security agreement describing the collateral"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 9-203, attachment requires three elements: (1) the secured party gives value, (2) the debtor has rights in the collateral, and (3) there is an authenticated security agreement describing the collateral. Attachment creates the security interest between the parties.
Wrong (A): Filing a UCC-1 financing statement is required for perfection under Section 9-310, not attachment.
Wrong (B): The debtor''s first payment is not a legal requirement for attachment under Article 9.
Wrong (C): A court judgment is a post-default enforcement remedy, not an element of attachment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1177;

-- Q1182: Debtor-Creditor Relationships [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The seller has priority because the March 18 PMSI filing falls within the 20-day grace period, granting retroactive super-priority; if filed on March 25 (day 24), priority would be lost","The seller loses priority regardless of timing because previously filed blanket security interests always defeat later-filed interests without any exception","The grace period for PMSI perfection is 30 days, so both a March 18 and a March 25 filing would preserve the seller''s super-priority over First National","The seller has priority only if the financing statement was filed before delivery, not after — the 20-day period runs before the delivery date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): UCC Section 9-324(a) grants a PMSI in goods (other than inventory) super-priority over conflicting security interests if perfected within 20 days of the debtor receiving possession. The March 18 filing (day 17) is within the window, giving the seller retroactive priority from the delivery date. If filed on March 25 (day 24), the grace period would expire and First National''s earlier filing would prevail.
Wrong (B): The PMSI super-priority is a specific exception to the first-to-file rule under the UCC.
Wrong (C): The grace period is 20 days under Section 9-324(a), not 30 days.
Wrong (D): The 20-day period runs after delivery, not before it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1182;

-- Q1263: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [hard/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both qualify as dependents because the gross income test does not apply to any person under age 24 regardless of student status","Neither qualifies because earning $18,000 automatically disqualifies anyone from dependent status under both qualifying child and qualifying relative tests","Karen qualifies as a qualifying child (no gross income test; student exception extends age limit to 24), while Alex fails as both qualifying child (age test) and qualifying relative (income test)","Karen qualifies as a qualifying relative while Alex qualifies as a qualifying child, because the student exception reverses which category each person falls under"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 152(c), a qualifying child has no gross income test. Karen satisfies the age test because Sec. 152(c)(3)(A)(ii) extends the limit from 19 to 24 for full-time students, so her $18,000 earnings are irrelevant. Alex, at 20 and not a student, fails the qualifying child age test and must be tested as a qualifying relative under Sec. 152(d), where his $18,000 exceeds the gross income threshold.
Wrong (A): The gross income test exemption applies only to qualifying children, not all persons under 24.
Wrong (B): Earnings do not automatically disqualify a qualifying child — only qualifying relatives face the income test.
Wrong (D): This reverses the categories — Karen is a qualifying child, not a qualifying relative.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1263;

-- Q1265: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [hard/L3]
