-- Q1266: Tax-Exempt Organizations [easy/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both activities are equally prohibited for 501(c)(3) organizations and will result in immediate loss of exempt status","Both activities are permitted without limitation because the First Amendment protects all nonprofit speech and advocacy activities","Lobbying is permitted in insubstantial amounts (or under the Section 501(h) expenditure test), while campaign intervention for a specific candidate is absolutely prohibited","Campaign intervention is permitted if it represents less than 5% of activities, while lobbying is absolutely prohibited regardless of amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 501(c)(3) draws a sharp line between lobbying and campaign activity. Lobbying (attempting to influence legislation) is permitted if not substantial, or under the Section 501(h) election with specific expenditure limits. Campaign intervention — supporting or opposing a candidate — is absolutely prohibited with no de minimis exception and can trigger loss of exempt status.
Wrong (A): Lobbying is permitted within limits; only campaign activity is absolutely prohibited.
Wrong (B): The tax code does impose restrictions on exempt organizations despite First Amendment protections.
Wrong (D): This reverses the rules — campaign activity has no safe harbor percentage, while lobbying is the permitted activity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1266;

