-- Q2393: Circular 230 [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No penalty because communications with the OPR during a disciplinary investigation are privileged and protected","A written warning with no further consequences beyond documentation of the false statement in the practitioner''s file","Automatic referral to criminal prosecution under federal obstruction statutes without any intermediate disciplinary step","Additional sanctions including an enhanced penalty and potential disbarment for obstructing the disciplinary investigation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under 31 CFR Section 10.51(a)(7), providing false or misleading information to the OPR during a disciplinary investigation is itself a sanctionable violation. This constitutes obstruction and can result in enhanced sanctions beyond those for the original misconduct, including disbarment under Section 10.50.
Wrong (A): Communications during OPR investigations carry an affirmative obligation of truthfulness and are not privileged.
Wrong (B): False statements to the OPR trigger sanctions far more severe than a written warning.
