-- Q2408: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA may prepare the current-year return because the prior-year error does not affect current-year amounts, but must document the client''s refusal and consider the relationship","The CPA must refuse to prepare any future returns for the client once any prior-year error is discovered, regardless of whether it affects the current year","The CPA may prepare the current-year return and carry forward the incorrect amounts because the error originated in a year the CPA did not prepare","The CPA must report the client to the IRS before preparing any current-year return to avoid potential preparer penalties"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSTS No. 6, when a prior-year error has no carryforward effect on the current return, the CPA may continue preparing the current return because no incorrect amounts flow into the current year. The CPA should document the client''s refusal to amend and evaluate the relationship. If the error did affect carryforward items (basis, NOLs under IRC Sec. 172), the CPA could not sign a return with known incorrect amounts.
Wrong (B): SSTS No. 6 does not require refusing all future returns — only those where errors would carry forward.
Wrong (C): A CPA cannot knowingly carry forward incorrect amounts regardless of who prepared the prior return.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 6 does not require reporting the client to the IRS; confidentiality obligations apply.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2408;

-- Q2410: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA has no responsibility because the software vendor assumes all liability for calculation errors in the return","The CPA is responsible only if the CPA personally wrote or modified the tax preparation software used for the return","The CPA should review the return for reasonableness and accuracy and is responsible for the proper use of software in the preparation process","The CPA must independently verify every calculation on the return by hand before filing to satisfy due diligence standards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SSTS No. 3 (Certain Procedural Aspects of Preparing Returns), a CPA may rely on tax preparation software for mathematical accuracy but retains professional responsibility to review the output for reasonableness. This includes verifying that the software correctly applied tax rates, limitations, and phase-outs.
Wrong (A): The CPA retains professional responsibility regardless of whether software produced the error.
Wrong (B): Responsibility is not limited to cases where the CPA wrote the software — it applies to all return preparation.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 3 requires a reasonableness review, not manual verification of every individual calculation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2410;

