Wrong (B): No $1,000 threshold exists — the decision depends on professional judgment about complexity and risk.
Wrong (C): A long-standing client relationship does not reduce the need for written advice on complex matters.
Wrong (D): Written advice considerations apply broadly to complex matters, not only to listed transactions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2417;

-- Q2420: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is never acceptable because IRS regulations cannot be challenged on a tax return under any circumstances","Requires pre-approval from the IRS National Office before the position may be taken on the client''s return","Is acceptable only if the taxpayer has previously won on the identical issue in a Tax Court proceeding","May be acceptable if the CPA has a good-faith belief that the regulation does not accurately reflect the statutory law"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SSTS No. 1 and Circular 230 Section 10.34, a CPA may recommend a position contrary to an IRS regulation if supported by substantial authority (such as case law or statutory language) and a good-faith belief that the regulation does not accurately reflect the law. The CPA should consider disclosure requirements under IRC Section 6662.
Wrong (A): Regulations are not immune from challenge — positions with substantial authority may contradict them.
Wrong (B): No pre-approval from the IRS National Office is required to take a contrary position.
Wrong (C): A prior Tax Court victory is not a prerequisite; substantial authority from any recognized source suffices.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2420;

-- Q2422: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Report the client to the appropriate licensing authority because operating without a license violates state regulatory requirements","Continue to prepare the return accurately and advise the client to obtain proper licensing, as tax reporting is separate from regulatory compliance","Include a note on the return disclosing the unlicensed status to the IRS as a material fact affecting the income reported","Refuse to report the income from the unlicensed business because including illegal income could implicate the CPA"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under SSTS No. 1 and IRC Section 61(a), all income from whatever source derived is includable in gross income, regardless of whether the underlying activity is legally licensed. The CPA''s obligation is to report the income accurately and advise the client to obtain proper licensing.
Wrong (A): The CPA is not required to report the client to a licensing authority; confidentiality obligations apply.
Wrong (C): The IRS does not require disclosure of licensing status on the return — only accurate income reporting.
Wrong (D): Income from unlicensed activities is still taxable under Section 61(a) and must be reported.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2422;

