-- Q2429: Tax Procedures [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The three-year statute applies regardless of the omission amount because there is no provision extending the limitations period for income omissions","Any omission of any amount automatically extends the statute to six years because all unreported income errors receive identical treatment","The $30,000 omission exceeds 25% of reported gross income ($25,000 threshold), triggering the six-year statute; a $20,000 omission would fall below the threshold at 20%","The 25% threshold reduces the statute to one year rather than extending it, because smaller omissions receive longer scrutiny periods"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 6501(e)(1)(A) extends the assessment period from three to six years when the taxpayer omits more than 25% of gross income stated on the return. With $100,000 reported and $30,000 omitted, the ratio is 30%, exceeding the 25% threshold. At $20,000, the ratio would be 20%, below the threshold, and the standard three-year period would apply.
Wrong (A): Section 6501(e) specifically provides an extended period for substantial omissions of gross income.
Wrong (B): The six-year extension requires exceeding the 25% threshold — not every omission triggers it.
Wrong (D): This reverses the effect entirely; the 25% threshold extends the period, it does not shorten it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2429;

-- Q2439: Tax Procedures [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The later of three years from filing or two years from the date the tax was paid, which determines the maximum refund window","One year from the date of overpayment, which is the standard refund claim period for all taxpayers","Five years from the due date of the return, which applies to refund claims regardless of when the return was filed","There is no time limit for refund claims because the government should always return overpaid tax amounts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6511, a taxpayer must file a refund claim by the later of three years from the date the return was filed or two years from the date the tax was paid. The refund amount is limited based on which window applies — three years of payments or two years of payments, respectively.
Wrong (B): One year is not a recognized refund claim period under Section 6511.
Wrong (C): The statute uses three years from filing date, not five years from the due date.
Wrong (D): Refund claims are subject to strict time limits under Section 6511; there is no unlimited window.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2439;

-- Q2443: Tax Procedures [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is calculated as a flat 5% of the total underpayment amount owed when the return is filed","Does not apply if the total tax owed when filing is less than $10,000 for the taxable year","Can be waived only if the taxpayer has never previously underpaid estimated taxes in any prior year","Is calculated using the federal short-term rate plus 3 percentage points, applied to each quarterly underpayment for the period of underpayment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 6654, the estimated tax penalty is computed as interest on the underpayment at the federal short-term rate plus 3 percentage points, calculated for each quarter separately from the quarterly due date to the earlier of the payment date or filing deadline. Safe harbors exist (paying 90% of current-year tax or 100%/110% of prior-year tax).
Wrong (A): The penalty is not a flat 5% — it is an interest-based calculation tied to the federal short-term rate.
Wrong (B): No $10,000 exemption threshold exists; the penalty applies based on the quarterly underpayment amount.
Wrong (C): The penalty may be waived for casualty, disaster, or unusual circumstances — not limited to first-time underpayers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2443;

-- Q2450: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A manifestation of present contractual intent, definite and certain terms, and communication of the offer to the offeree","A written document signed by both parties, which is required for all valid offers under common law principles","Consideration exchanged at the time the offer is made, establishing mutual obligation before acceptance can occur","Approval by a neutral third party who verifies that the proposed terms are fair and enforceable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts, Section 24, a valid offer requires (1) a manifestation of present contractual intent, (2) definite and certain terms, and (3) communication to the offeree. Garrett''s proposal fails because "a reasonable price to be determined later" lacks definiteness in a material term.
Wrong (B): A written document is not required for a valid offer unless the Statute of Frauds applies.
Wrong (C): Consideration is required for a binding contract, not for the validity of an offer itself.
Wrong (D): No third-party approval requirement exists for valid offers under common law.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2450;
